WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.331
Hello, we're the SCOPE-XR team, part of NSF
VINES Track 2.

2
00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:16.878
Our team brings together the University of
Georgia, UT Austin, Virginia Tech, UW–Madison,
Ericsson, and OpenAirInterface.

3
00:00:17.128 --> 00:00:24.051
We're using what extended reality headsets see
to help wireless networks keep people
connected as they move.

4
00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:30.415
In a wireless networking lesson, students
could use XR to see the signals around them.

5
00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:37.101
They could explore how signals travel through
a room, reflect off walls, and leave gaps in
coverage.

6
00:00:37.351 --> 00:00:43.074
For industrial safety training, the connection
needs to hold as people move around equipment.

7
00:00:43.324 --> 00:00:49.891
As someone moves behind an obstacle, the
network could switch to a reflected path to
keep them connected.

8
00:00:50.141 --> 00:00:56.176
Both examples point to the same need:
understanding the room and keeping each
headset connected.

9
00:00:56.976 --> 00:01:00.334
SCOPE-XR brings that understanding into the
network.

10
00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:05.517
We start by combining the headset's view of
the room with a few radio measurements.

11
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:11.877
That gives us a model of how signals travel,
including where connections may become
unreliable.

12
00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:18.094
We also consider what people are doing and
where they're looking, so the right content
gets priority.

13
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:24.469
Together, these inputs help the network choose
signal paths and decide how to share its
capacity.

14
00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:29.808
The headset, the network, and the application
can then respond to changes together.

15
00:01:30.058 --> 00:01:34.152
We'll connect these capabilities through
O-RAN's open architecture.

16
00:01:34.402 --> 00:01:39.554
Our network control will run as xApps for
beams, scheduling, and handovers.

17
00:01:39.803 --> 00:01:45.495
For longer-term optimization, rApps will
connect with Ericsson's automation platform.

18
00:01:45.745 --> 00:01:50.497
Standard interfaces will link our software to
commercial radio equipment.

19
00:01:51.297 --> 00:01:57.189
We'll begin with testbeds at UGA and Virginia
Tech, using commercial radio equipment.

20
00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:03.051
We'll test what happens when people move,
cross paths, or block one another's signals.

21
00:02:03.301 --> 00:02:07.776
Then we'll bring the pieces together on
Virginia Tech's larger testbed.

22
00:02:08.026 --> 00:02:16.453
The planned demonstrations will involve
several people sharing XR experiences across
rooms and indoor and outdoor spaces.

23
00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:23.456
For both learning and industrial safety, we'll
measure how responsive and reliable the
experience is,

24
00:02:23.706 --> 00:02:26.104
and how much computing it takes to support it.

25
00:02:26.904 --> 00:02:32.481
We'll share software, datasets, and evaluation
tools so others can build on the work.

26
00:02:32.731 --> 00:02:40.194
With Ericsson and OpenAirInterface, we'll work
toward reusable components that can be adopted
beyond our testbeds.

27
00:02:40.444 --> 00:02:43.457
We welcome collaboration with other VINES
teams.

28
00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:47.047
Learn more at scope-xr.github.io. Thank you.
